 | A revoked sign-in | A 401 whose words say the sign-in itself is gone (`SignInRevoked`: ChatGPT's `token_revoked`, `invalid_grant`, `refresh_token_reused`; never `token_expired`, a 403, a 5xx or a network error) marks the account for the credential it was sent with (`NoteRevokedSignIn`, #1793). A saved ChatGPT account is refreshed first, as Codex does on a 401, and a refresh that goes through marks nothing. The mark is a hash of the header, never the credential, kept in `logins.json` as `Refused: "revoked:<mark>"` with the account's `Lapsed`, and read back after a restart. While the account has that credential, the gateway doesn't send its requests (`RevokedSignIn`); its row says Sign-in required with Sign in again. Signed in anew (`upsertLogin` with another credential, `addSideLogin`), refreshed, or served a request (`ServedSignIn`), it is clear. Claude (`claude_auth.go`), Copilot (`copilot_lapsed.go`: GitHub's 401/403 on the token exchange, the CLI's stand-in or the usage read, cleared when a token is served) and plugins keep their own marks. | [`signin_revoked.go`](../../internal/provider/signin_revoked.go), [`copilot_lapsed.go`](../../internal/provider/copilot_lapsed.go) |
 | Saved accounts | `logins.json` remembers every Codex and Claude Code account seen signed in. A switch moves the credentials into the agent's own store and saves the ones it replaces. A saved account that is on (`SetLoginOn`) stands behind the agent's own. Its tokens stay in `logins.json`. | [`logins.go`](../../internal/provider/logins.go), [`logins_on.go`](../../internal/provider/logins_on.go) |
 | Sign-in in magpie | `StartSignIn` runs the vendor's own browser OAuth with its client and takes the tokens at a callback on this machine. A browser that can't reach that callback (magpie on a server or in Docker) ends on a page that won't load; its address, pasted (`SubmitSignInCallback`), goes through the same callback handler (`pastedCallback`). ChatGPT's sign-in comes back only to port 1455; when that port can't be opened, the sign-in still starts and waits for the pasted address alone, and says why in `CallbackBlocked` with the port in `CallbackPort`: `reserved` for Windows' WSAEACCES on a port in a range it excludes for Hyper-V, WSL or Docker, `busy` for anything else, such as another program holding it (`callbackBlocked`, #1782). A plugin's browser sign-in (`auto`) whose page names a port on this machine to come back to takes its pasted address too: magpie opens it there on 127.0.0.1, for the plugin's own listener (`pluginCallback`, `loopbackPorts`); Command Code's page posts its key from script, so it offers the API key way instead. A Claude account is signed in by Claude Code itself. Signing in brings back an account removed from magpie (`ShowAccount`). A sign-in started in magpie (`StartSignIn`, `StartPluginSignIn`) reads as done (`SignInStatus`, `WaitSignIn`) only after `finish` has run `ShowAccount` for its account, so the window, which opens the account as soon as it sees done, finds it listed. If providers.json can't be written then, the account stays hidden and the sign-in still ends done. Signing in to a plugin's provider from the command line (`pluginLogin`, with a key or the browser) brings it back too. It also takes off the mark a sign-in its vendor turned away left on the account (`Lapsed`) and shows again the agent's own account removed in magpie (`clearPluginLapse`). The window's plugin sign-in does the same with the browser (`pluginDone`, then `finish`) or with a key (`PluginAPIKey`); a key and the command line share these steps in `PluginSignedIn`. A sign-in leaves through the proxy the account's own requests will use (#1663): `ProxyOfLogin` (the account's, else the subscription's, else the global one) for the account being signed in again, the subscription's for a new one. Every request of the flow goes through `signInFlow.via`, Claude Code's own login gets it as *_PROXY (`netproxy.EnvWith`), and a plugin's sign-in runs its fetch under that proxy (`via` in `host.js`; `plugins_cli.go` for the command line). The window's sign-in box shows it as a "Proxy: …" link (`signInProxyLink`, the app menu, no userinfo shown) with Global, Direct, the subscription's and each account's proxy, or a typed one; picking one starts the sign-in again through it (`SignInOptions.Proxy`), and when it ends the pick is kept as the account's (`keepSignInProxy`: the subscription's own when it is its only account, else the account's entry). Global isn't offered while the subscription has a proxy and other accounts, since nothing could keep it. The browser's own leg goes through the browser's network, not magpie's. Signing out leaves through the same exit: a ChatGPT account's refresh token is revoked through its own proxy, read before the account is forgotten (`forgetSIWCLogin`), and Devin's CLI, asked who is signed in or for its models, runs with the account's proxy (`devinCommand`, `devinProxy`), or with the sign-in's while one asks it. | [`signin.go`](../../internal/provider/signin.go), [`claude_signin.go`](../../internal/provider/claude_signin.go), [`pluginsignin.go`](../../internal/provider/pluginsignin.go), [`pluginsignin_paste.go`](../../internal/provider/pluginsignin_paste.go), [`proxy.go`](../../internal/provider/proxy.go), [`plugins_cli.go`](../../internal/cli/plugins_cli.go) |
+| Import from apps | `ImportSources` previews CC Switch and Alma entries read-only; `ImportFromApps` rereads and saves only the picks. A CC Switch Gemini entry's `GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL`, or Alma's `gemini` API format or `google` type with a base, becomes a custom provider's `Gemini` endpoint with its key and models. Missing keys, invalid bases and magpie's own address remain skipped; a Google entry with no custom base keeps the Google preset. | [`importapps.go`](../../internal/provider/importapps.go), [`importapps_test.go`](../../internal/provider/importapps_test.go) |
 | Import from files | `ImportLogins` (ChatGPT, Claude), `ImportGoogleAccounts` (Antigravity) and `ImportFactoryKeys` take other tools' files, from the window (`signin/import`) or `magpie accounts import` (#1453). ChatGPT reads Codex's `auth.json`, CLIProxyAPI's and Cockpit Tools' files, Sub2API's export (`sub2apiEntry`) and codexbar's `~/.codexbar/config.json` (`codexbarEntries`: the `openai_oauth` provider's accounts and `openAI.remoteConnectionAccounts`, the workspace from `openAIAccountId`). Each ChatGPT and Google sign-in is refreshed before it is added, so the tool the file came from is signed out of it; both surfaces say so first, and the command imports nothing without `--yes` or a yes. Claude reads CLIProxyAPI's files and Sub2API's export, whose Claude account says its email as Anthropic does, `email_address`, in `credentials` or `extra`, and keeps its `scope`, `account_uuid` and `org_uuid` as Claude Code's scopes, `accountUuid` and `organizationUuid` (#1975); a file with no email is refused, as Claude Code's own `.credentials.json` is. Claude's are kept as the file has them. A refresh token magpie holds already is `exists`, never refreshed; accounts are matched by email and workspace, or for Claude organization (`sameLogin`). An entry with an access token and no refresh token is refused (`accessOnly`). One file of several that holds no account is a failed row with its place (`ImportedAccount.File`), never skipped. The files are only read. | [`login_import.go`](../../internal/provider/login_import.go), [`google_import.go`](../../internal/provider/google_import.go), [`factory_key.go`](../../internal/provider/factory_key.go), [`accounts_cli.go`](../../internal/cli/accounts_cli.go) |
